f 17592186049374 :topic/id #uuid "efecc103-69a4-5160-9e24-2c7502edddb5" :topic/math-academy-id 3275} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fe74356-49b8-543c-8d6e-d896f6be8ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$7$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442821} {:answer/id #uuid "894f80a8-661c-52a9-bf68-6f0f805aa1ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442827} {:answer/id #uuid "991ebd97-1bba-53d6-8eb0-37ded272c11a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ week" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442830} {:answer/id #uuid "ac92feca-9b24-5ba3-8997-1c3b5fdc560b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442832} {:answer/id #uuid "bba18b49-af2d-51f5-a5ee-8ea2b7824778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$7$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442833}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442832} :answer-field/id #uuid "972739f9-2ca7-5e51-8be2-cdc627574abe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7ced14e8-8144-5a90-a159-85ee1ea70744" :question/math-academy-id "q-138314" :question/problem "The time $X,$ in weeks, between two grandkids visiting their grandma can be modeled as an exponential random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(7).$ If a grandkid has just visited their grandma, what is the average time until the next grandkid visits their grandma?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(7).$ Therefore, $\\lambda =7,$ which means that grandkids visit their grandma at an average rate of $7$ visits per week. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{week}=1\\,\\text{day}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average time until the next grandkid's visit is $1$ day."} :step/id #uuid "5cd54c92-39ad-5b9a-a09b-fe3547af99e6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442848}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442846 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13aa5558-5072-53e7-bd58-cf7c7ba5a133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$15$ seconds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442819} {:answer/id #uuid "33219482-ef34-597b-b10a-a5cf5d960dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442822} {:answer/id #uuid "530f6bf8-4fcb-5c94-9cee-6007d643f0f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442824} {:answer/id #uuid "8acfbc95-a05b-5d37-8140-8a62e5da7a2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$30$ seconds" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442828} {:answer/id #uuid "d06dab70-fbd9-57eb-9947-fb23ab39c34e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442834}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442834} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7049d2d-fc9f-5f20-8e5c-02896d997034" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bbd9b202-0edb-5252-b84c-96bc38834bd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132627" :question/problem "The time $X,$ in minutes, between two visitors entering a restaurant can be modeled as an exponential random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{2}).$ If a visitor has just entered the restaurant, what is the average time until the next visitor enters?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{2}).$ Therefore, $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{min}=2\\,\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average time until the next visitor is $2$ minutes."} :step/id #uuid "778e6bc3-396f-57e9-ad00-0591e91cecbb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fdcd7a-935f-5b89-954e-94cfe53551b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442818} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec31d2e-690a-53af-8152-9fa7d7a9b34e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442823} {:answer/id #uuid "6af5ae31-0114-5767-80ce-a1faf0e59131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442826} {:answer/id #uuid "d09b5346-0b32-599f-8cfd-4d40bae72ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442835} {:answer/id #uuid "f43ab765-767d-579c-9eae-df2ab5f23f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442818} :answer-field/id #uuid "381abdbc-0501-57da-9b23-4cb39840288c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0dc4b9d8-5da7-5586-a518-10322d637373" :question/math-academy-id "q-132362" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),$ what is $\\text{Var}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d0c54f8f-efb1-5756-b356-6ea276ef3ad3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442846}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442848 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05547d8f-b729-55a9-8be3-036d66839167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442817} {:answer/id #uuid "2520da57-e0e2-58df-b3cf-ec9420890983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442820} {:answer/id #uuid "611a9703-d8be-5d7c-b587-568f34b6ac1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442825} {:answer/id #uuid "902763c5-4b29-5719-bd61-94b503ffbf10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442829} {:answer/id #uuid "9f233499-e299-5d78-8480-e3546726af7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442831}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442831} :answer-field/id #uuid "8efe295c-e280-5ef7-a7b5-b62fe5c12fd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "358a711e-17d9-56a3-a7f2-d4538d452099" :question/math-academy-id "q-138321" :question/problem "The time $X,$ in days, between two doses of medication in a patient's treatment can be modeled as an exponential random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(6).$ If a dose has just been taken by the patient, what is the average time until the next dose?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(6).$ Therefore, $\\lambda =6,$ which means that $6$ doses are taken each day. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{day}=4\\,\\text{h}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average time until the next dose is $4$ hours."} :step/id #uuid "d1a1ba80-2f4c-549a-969a-7ffdffc5fabd"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442849 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1608335-135d-518f-b0f8-9d14f1148e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163547} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4d0544-6ad8-5103-8c56-091eafd7f5a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163548} {:answer/id #uuid "049f0e31-087f-545b-a089-f313f3063bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163549} {:answer/id #uuid "0e250c9e-c165-578b-9fe5-a70487768653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163550} {:answer/id #uuid "23e3423b-695d-56f2-9c74-65be2374abc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163551}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163550} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b75e96e-6286-569e-8a97-9f97357e7c1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b606a84e-9f53-5e58-b319-b9877d0150ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-112759" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(0.2),$ what is $\\text{Var}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(0.2),$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{1}{{(0.2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0.04} \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ec507f4e-7ad4-574b-b220-b028ea07092f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442847}}] :activity/title "Mean and Variance of the Exponential Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442816}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491232} :activity/id #uuid "47cf4fd6-a08e-5043-8ff7-4d0c5079766c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049374 :topic/id #uuid "efecc103-69a4-5160-9e24-2c7502edddb5" :topic/math-academy-id 3275} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491219 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cae4c83-306c-5bf5-afb5-e4bc848532bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$10$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490622} {:answer/id #uuid "4c341e79-a06b-56f0-934a-8cb70addaf6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490657} {:answer/id #uuid "89cf7e83-9197-575b-a03c-befeab00a774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490786} {:answer/id #uuid "e5967682-42a1-56b0-aeab-ef5ac5a9ddcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490949} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c5fdb9-71a5-5842-806e-387f12f8736d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$10$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490985}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490985} :answer-field/id #uuid "b89135c1-e626-5a50-b92b-10a362d39109" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "63be50ef-0747-5e60-8532-f2bbd0fe71fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-132622" :question/problem "The time $X,$ in hours, between two smartphones being purchased at a particular store can be modeled as an exponential random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(0.1).$ If a smartphone is just being purchased in this store, what is the average time until the next smartphone is purchased?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(0.1).$ Therefore, $\\lambda =0.1,$ and\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{0.1}\\,\\text{hours}=10\\,\\text{hours}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average time until the next smartphone is purchased is $10$ hours."} :step/id #uuid "034cf39f-21a0-52ca-b145-e27e4f9c0e99" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491264}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491229 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4821eb28-1e88-51ad-aad4-f7dfdfec6587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490646} {:answer/id #uuid "4a63109f-beee-5a5f-8861-1aea21eee120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490653} {:answer/id #uuid "4f92c2ad-9f48-554d-9500-741e357a8e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490665} {:answer/id #uuid "91460a8c-4330-566f-8171-430847261e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490805} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1b866a-2690-5cf8-9106-66302437993d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490840}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490805} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ae81741-3a66-5168-8794-e804d129e9ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f1130bc7-7e29-58ea-ab37-f4439fc1b9bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-132351" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(2),$ what is $\\text{Var}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(2),$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{1}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "33d9787c-d276-5c89-8a81-ddb268f3145c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491260}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491232 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "add54bfd-543a-5753-842c-806a8f3c602f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163553} {:answer/id #uuid "0eb56243-475d-53dd-bd19-8062bca07351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163554} {:answer/id #uuid "1d15200f-78ce-5af9-aece-7bebe6ca55b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163555} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5437f9-a448-5de8-84c8-14215def4f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163556} {:answer/id #uuid "56588e37-c312-5153-8f13-a140aa3684be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163557}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163553} :answer-field/id #uuid "22978579-9ad3-5768-9161-b08345ec4e67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "44513738-6b05-51d6-ae93-8872c1f3b0c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-112760" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{5}{6}),$ what is $\\text{SD}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{5}{6}),$ we have the following standard deviation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3f3c3716-04c4-52c7-b7d0-78f31e2c3838" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491229}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491260 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f2dbe7-4d9f-58eb-bb49-d5b0449c485f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490523} {:answer/id #uuid "0ddd79ea-c919-58eb-b2f1-9dcb609b95b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490536} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc593e7-d496-5234-93d3-64dcd1a6e4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490623} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca50db0-0996-5ec6-a159-57c6549064dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490722} {:answer/id #uuid "c3247857-d9d4-5eb9-bffe-4aec28e750da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490886}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490722} :answer-field/id #uuid "20fb49a8-932a-5d90-bedd-3c4b3ee5a705" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e121d225-86ab-5e4f-995c-5ee729943442" :question/math-academy-id "q-132360" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{4}),$ what is $\\text{Var}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{4}),$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{16})} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b1aa3e8c-36be-560d-9843-597bdb08b822" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491219}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491264 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7af9c393-6c4b-5ea5-8d8e-e6ecae34f646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490755} {:answer/id #uuid "87328952-5e76-5226-8b75-e1e77055ba16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$7.5$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490781} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec0aa02-7069-52f5-bf76-34f8ddb54455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3.5$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490798} {:answer/id #uuid "e30bf5b3-8a5a-5ac6-afa4-778b217478ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490944} {:answer/id #uuid "eeabcfc5-2e58-5fd3-9715-75364be6e3a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490967}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490781} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bae4bea-360d-51cc-8f0f-ab3d756e9cef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2edecaf4-e99a-5cf4-8897-cf987c19074c" :question/math-academy-id "q-132626" :question/problem "The time $X,$ in hours, between two customers buying an anniversary card at a gift store can be modeled as an exponential random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(8).$ If a customer has just bought an anniversary card, what is the average time until the next customer buys an anniversary card?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(8).$ Therefore, $\\lambda =8,$ which means that $8$ customers buy an anniversary card each hour. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{h}=7.5\\,\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average time until the next customer buys an anniversary card is $7.5$ minutes."} :step/id #uuid "c3801fb8-114b-5472-ad17-7b699337b0f6"}] :activity/title "Mean and Variance of the Exponential Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490501}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129963} :activity/id #uuid "0e238e7b-3085-5b87-b3c4-0f5c35258e51" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050820 :topic/id #uuid "6902b215-0b25-5d7a-a72f-3376ac4e0554" :topic/math-academy-id 1977} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130347 :tutorial/content "Consider the following $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n![](images/4f/4fd591d8028847aa7e1d11e840acdb192703d152f9c397e40fec98fd1e281c41.svg)\n\nIf we are told that one of the eigenvalues of this matrix is $\\lambda = 3,$ how do we calculate the corresponding eigenvector?\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this reduced matrix, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have $x_3 = 0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain $x_2 = 0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/70/705dd92c7330897f35671b66866791160b9d7f65e3280d5ccb9d992405c08942.svg)\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](images/2a/2a5dd6c35fb81134f13e765e0a02be4ea2feedfd878a1923491813492739ef00.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "b1a9d148-15f7-5878-8f36-f19ee99c49d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3566 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "875dc5aa-e4f4-50f8-a30e-d5ed46d77e32" :step/math-academy-id 20099 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129964}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129127 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128938 :question/id #uuid "d6c73b7b-4981-5908-b694-be7f0e57b612" :question/math-academy-id "e-5490"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c79885-7848-51e3-9f96-6a109e776fd5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455512}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "59537f37-7060-5544-9b4a-84501e450c50" :step/math-academy-id 20100 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129128 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128939 :question/id #uuid "b087e601-c054-563c-b7ee-43e18c74ac51" :question/math-academy-id "e-5491"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431011}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "8a926ac2-afd2-5b57-8911-c3b8dac86d39" :step/math-academy-id 20101 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130084}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129129 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128940 :question/id #uuid "c22ab531-3377-5827-93d2-a13854b3308c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5492"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e732a2b-a6a1-5e0f-b43e-a9fbc02cfc74" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538983}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "5d1fcdf4-d676-5ff5-bf31-d7e371f43f08" :step/math-academy-id 20102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130403 :tutorial/content "Note the following important theorem.\n\n*The set of eigenvectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_k \\}$ of a matrix $A,$ where each vector $\\mathbf v_i$ corresponds to a different eigenvalue, is linearly independent.*\n\n**Watch out!** Eigenvectors that correspond to the *same* eigenvalue *can* be (and very often *will* be) linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "c0f0f6d3-c9e1-52a6-bf51-244bb7751a9d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4432 :tutorial/title "Eigenvectors That Correspond to Different Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "3314b023-f15c-5f8b-a4e4-aeea702377a2" :step/math-academy-id 27265 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129966}}] :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129325}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477000} :activity/id #uuid "3ab8d004-5a46-5aac-836a-cb6aba195154" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050820 :topic/id #uuid "6902b215-0b25-5d7a-a72f-3376ac4e0554" :topic/math-academy-id 1977} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5988b748-db6c-5b36-9840-e6478cabbb51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321853} {:answer/id #uuid "7c472c58-699c-5782-89ce-b06e067314de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322125} {:answer/id #uuid "cf3e7fab-ed01-5f57-aecb-974f3540bbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322766} {:answer/id #uuid "d0690c0d-0bea-5716-9be4-2e7a0d6ed043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322774} {:answer/id #uuid "dc30c442-053c-588a-a4c2-b47c4d9a660e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322766} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb178049-21d1-5f35-85d8-2469af417f05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8f655022-c86d-5349-9391-8c2ff380546e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37403" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{20}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=4{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2(4{x}_{3})-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-4{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{-4}{1}=-4.$"} :step/id #uuid "55c470c3-9a2c-5b79-a7cb-6bb99638869c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476990}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476990 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "963f3713-419f-5a42-a8fd-36820ed2b9d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399243} {:answer/id #uuid "77d0fe15-2bfd-57f0-991d-684ad2dfd05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399244} {:answer/id #uuid "a30c29de-8bbd-5ce5-b082-0d86e79ae9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399245} {:answer/id #uuid "c4de3ee5-4e26-5981-b4bd-fa52650fe448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399246} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0fd701-7b05-5e6d-9f51-cb1531e040d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399247} :answer-field/id #uuid "00acfb45-f915-59a3-94c8-f02ca6e1865b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "896ad0b2-d79e-5667-ae9b-69d97f290fa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-89417" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4{x}_{3}-2{x}_{3}) \\\\  & ={x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "7cbd70ad-da38-5750-9d78-fe45b60fc21e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476999}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d27b92-6f03-55bd-b6a9-1d288bcf8c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454911} {:answer/id #uuid "2de3eabf-cab8-5591-a492-a96a3f773d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454970} {:answer/id #uuid "4d120a10-5675-5201-91f6-2be8ae41d759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455036} {:answer/id #uuid "5c95a6d3-17be-5589-ac03-00cd648b9d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455069} {:answer/id #uuid "9812912f-0bf4-5fe1-b7d3-e532d4e7dbdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455178}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454911} :answer-field/id #uuid "061976f0-d2ab-56b5-ae57-17c4949d29c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b92ae563-4565-5947-89cb-f058a9c4a4a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-37398" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -14{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 9 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 10 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "bd53a707-c6f5-5559-ae54-26e649e0c977" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476985}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290f4389-144e-58ae-8aa0-f8779961574c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355007} {:answer/id #uuid "426fd857-13b1-5ab3-9758-9722e35fc330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355251} {:answer/id #uuid "435fb70d-dab8-537c-a107-ef7870828b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355266} {:answer/id #uuid "994c1016-eda3-584d-9ca2-ccfb245592f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356025} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2df7b5-bcfb-56b5-bfec-d5975826e4f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356741}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355266} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f55dc89-7192-5755-9048-0d366538fc46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "761e0b9c-f7fe-532d-9428-c3e7936605ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-89427" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-4-\\lambda  & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-4-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )((-4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-4)(-4)) & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -24) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +6)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-6.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-6.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-6.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-6)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+8{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=-{x}_{3}.$ From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "eb3f1758-bf5d-59f0-9e9b-84fea9836cfa"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033fe036-222d-5d10-957e-6471d19dc5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321179} {:answer/id #uuid "2c482aed-cfdd-5bf4-acda-648b685f18fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321489} {:answer/id #uuid "2d64e163-f5c4-5413-9570-dd5493a5c5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321500} {:answer/id #uuid "c7bd880a-2b37-5ebe-b42b-b4c304a5a933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322697} {:answer/id #uuid "e11d49f5-3c92-5fce-b032-36bf3f972d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322906}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322906} :answer-field/id #uuid "52e35bc9-d423-567e-8c0e-63b05f91b6ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4fce4c9f-341f-5617-8150-093fe040d196" :question/math-academy-id "q-37385" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-2)(-2)) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=0.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "f596f2be-8e67-5ffa-bdef-b19f8d8da98f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476994}}] :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476198}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539070} :activity/id #uuid "129a1854-2f4b-567e-bfbe-6c1b7fe4b689" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050820 :topic/id #uuid "6902b215-0b25-5d7a-a72f-3376ac4e0554" :topic/math-academy-id 1977} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2118b9d8-3a94-5241-ac9a-03c1a8165254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381113} {:answer/id #uuid "7efa2077-efeb-556a-bdf6-a527627aa068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381114} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe1eecd-6744-563e-a0ca-489b943e4e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381115} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5cac8f-2fc0-5759-807f-d9ee28d2044f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381116} {:answer/id #uuid "c81fa2d6-7df9-5381-b5a7-f75a91e5988d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381113} :answer-field/id #uuid "b84bb508-82f8-5a30-b1a6-92bf25dfe082" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c980d806-f256-5388-b798-65b34624c78e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140722" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )[(3-\\lambda )^{2}-1] & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +8) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-(2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{3}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "54a37441-fb97-5482-8800-e7be9eb1f718"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13304c66-59ed-5804-b519-223c3709ae32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392040} {:answer/id #uuid "317f7bc8-17c3-5b65-8500-5bfb85e37bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392041} {:answer/id #uuid "808dfd35-c7cf-5560-bba9-dfef1206fd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392042} {:answer/id #uuid "94090702-def0-5eac-8760-9a3b321889de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392043} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb0fced-9501-56c9-8088-68d6df8fb853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392044}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392042} :answer-field/id #uuid "60b86f5a-a5c7-5a8b-9d41-513f5f26be40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ad9f3c50-0a7e-56b3-becb-281cac541291" :question/math-academy-id "q-36973" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a lower triangular matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =6.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -11 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-11{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -11 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -11 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "79acdebf-dccc-5a6c-84a3-c4482c1dbcac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26853257-a15d-56bd-baf3-05cb4af2b356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321448} {:answer/id #uuid "6215dc3a-949b-5a88-b400-1754de3451a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321914} {:answer/id #uuid "8f37a9ee-9183-5f94-b9e6-589f18d0723c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322284} {:answer/id #uuid "97007dd8-0a0c-5e5d-b2fe-2c703399817a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322352} {:answer/id #uuid "e831241f-8157-56bb-8b58-0b3139bec9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321448} :answer-field/id #uuid "001c22af-200a-5e74-a872-4fc6a4c58849" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b75fe299-3a00-56ce-ac2b-3a6b8472e075" :question/math-academy-id "q-37401" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(3)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{12}{5}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "9de96552-9aff-59f8-bf73-97a5a9a29e02" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539107}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3279f5ec-b5ad-574c-aebf-693bd2fdaa1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269730} {:answer/id #uuid "653c963e-8cbf-558c-a076-0707a4bd7a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270171} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7e2990-b7e7-5be9-a37d-ee55358620c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270375} {:answer/id #uuid "81fcddb5-5383-56b8-bcf4-c993974f8e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270392} {:answer/id #uuid "ca767cd0-e2d5-5f15-b839-31ed41909f67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271045}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186270392} :answer-field/id #uuid "87f091c3-7851-5215-bc6b-9b792d0722c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "00737dc0-f397-573a-80e1-d038ce87150d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140717" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={x}_{2}+2(0) \\\\  & ={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "be19b29e-d4c3-5ad8-ba5e-8d63063a62a1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539060}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88495c5e-727c-5bdc-8c66-f5f7a1da7068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399189} {:answer/id #uuid "973ba97a-cf4d-57f4-b6a7-6d7f845916bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399190} {:answer/id #uuid "a9de477f-3e83-5cb8-ab8c-3547e66a97cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399191} {:answer/id #uuid "89aba4dc-e9ba-5704-a200-86c4f6f35692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399192} {:answer/id #uuid "1965bab2-64b2-5353-bc34-d0701180dfe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399193}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399189} :answer-field/id #uuid "adc9838b-0769-5bf4-b3d7-c8c2300681dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ae635709-cef0-5d90-bcf8-39aaac1bc474" :question/math-academy-id "q-83480" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -9{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{9}{5}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & ={x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "e095c51e-c500-590a-8101-841f07aafbc2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539096}}] :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538218}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1320.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120942} :activity/id #uuid "c4822993-aaa8-5b57-a495-e95fef0a8388" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051040 :topic/id #uuid "7c8d2ba6-2860-5111-b509-9ce2c7360cf0" :topic/math-academy-id 1788} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120942 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120980 :tutorial/content "One application of differential equations is to model the current in electrical circuits.\n\nAn **electrical circuit** is a closed, continuous path through which an electric charge can flow. The **current**, measured in amperes ($\\textrm{A}$), is the rate at which electric charge flows through the circuit as a function of time.\n\nElectrical circuits can contain many different components.\n\n- A **power source**, such as a **cell** (or **battery**), that supplies energy to the circuit by maintaining a potential difference (electromotive force, EMF), measured in volts ($\\textrm{V}$).\n\n![](images/14/14053a143ddd99905a32b8fac9ce6137630c7ccfeff34180e70f3213af90670c.png)\n\n- **Conducting paths**, such as wires, through which current carriers (electrons) flow.\n\n![](images/0f/0fe1adaaa7a0f9f35fe3db550580585cf147879815c7274241e1cc1f1ca0f8fa.png)\n\n- A **resistor** opposes the current and dissipates energy as heat. Resistance is measured in ohms ($\\Omega$).\n\n![](images/f1/f171d1d5c60acd048f3427dd5bc3119f452c07dee7a3068875646e052a077f49.png)\n\n- An **inductor** opposes *changes* in the current over time by storing energy in a magnetic field. Inductance is measured in henries ($\\textrm{H}$).\n\n![](images/f6/f63114660eb67da4eb5df7592afeb4e02c3668818c9cfc1722d7fadb2415c204.png)\n\nIn the next slide, we will connect these components in series to form an RL circuit." :tutorial/id #uuid "1f085c58-a3ab-5386-b857-365fbf6f8696" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14568 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7f52605e-6a3c-5b85-9c20-17d25d7c35d3" :step/math-academy-id 589205 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120960}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120943 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120981 :tutorial/content "Consider the RL circuit shown below.  \n\n![](images/7a/7a0d33d5753469532efca3d7bd5bc62e7797a040dde61d7e94423dec829fa589.png)\n\nLet's find the differential equation that describes the current $I$ (in amperes) as a function of time $t$ (in seconds).\n\nThe diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor with resistance $15\\,\\Omega,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $3\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 15\\,\\Omega{:}$\n\n$$\nV_R = 15I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 3\\,\\textrm{H}{:}$\n\n$$\nV_L = 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5 = 15I + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5I = \\dfrac{5}{3}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "515104d6-5d9a-5a57-a4f5-b25826666786" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14572 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "d401f32c-c875-58bb-bc9f-0373d3944885" :step/math-academy-id 589206 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120944}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120944 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119962 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119542 :question/id #uuid "9be480e2-68b0-591c-b331-0751a225122c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21758"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11687da5-3a8e-56cb-adec-e3fa5cadf490" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048746}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441935}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit"} :step/id #uuid "8a75cdaf-9cea-5ed6-a4a5-e52dd16330a3" :step/math-academy-id 589207 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120945}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120945 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120984 :tutorial/content "In a first-order linear differential equation, the solution splits into a **transient term** that decays over time and a **steady-state term** that remains as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nIn an RL circuit with a constant electromotive force (EMF), this steady-state value corresponds to the long-term current in the circuit after all transient effects have died out, independent of the initial condition.\n\nTo demonstrate how to extract the steady-state current from the general solution of an RL circuit model, suppose the current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RL circuit governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 6I = 12.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 6I_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t) = Ae^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $I_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0 + 6\\alpha = 12\n  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  \\alpha = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $I_p(t) = 2.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-6t} + 2.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c = Ae^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The constant $I_p = 2$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to\\infty,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state current is $2$ amperes." :tutorial/id #uuid "28b9a8ae-9d65-5781-82b1-a5dbde3ac5ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14581 :tutorial/title "Steady-State Solutions for Constant EMFs"} :step/id #uuid "c18fa2c7-f455-52a1-9d15-2af89c7b3387" :step/math-academy-id 589210 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120946}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120946 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119963 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119541 :question/id #uuid "c7f85c6d-a7d6-5ae6-b82b-7e8608b6411e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21757"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88990b12-e7e0-5e21-9000-45357c07d04d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049543}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486376}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Constant EMF"} :step/id #uuid "89feae66-82e8-5242-981f-f9b7e94d4e27" :step/math-academy-id 589211 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120947}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120947 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120983 :tutorial/content "In many cases, the EMF produced by a power source is sinusoidal, not constant. Such a power source is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/43/4398148f91eda2bae1392f79e868e10c8967ecc23ca9f26b791bc1fbacc45ff9.png)\n\nIn a first-order linear RL circuit driven by a sinusoidal voltage, the solution consists of a transient current that decays over time and a steady-state current that oscillates with the same frequency as the input and is independent of the initial condition.\n\nFor example, let's find the amplitude of the steady-state current in the RL circuit shown below.\n\n![](images/59/59fd8a8db1a227a578670d2457011275446c87ba218686f8d1984f4a73a8cfa6.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\sin(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $7\\,\\Omega,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 7\\,\\Omega.$\n\n$$\nV_R = 7I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\n$$\nV_L = 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\sin(4t) = 7I + 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 7I = 3\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 7I_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t) = Ae^{-7t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+7(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(7\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +7\\beta &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 7\\alpha +4\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{12}{65},$ $\\beta = \\dfrac{21}{65}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = -\\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-7t} - \\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-7t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) =  - \\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t) \\approx I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{12}{65}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{21}{65}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.37\\,\\textrm{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. This amplitude represents the maximum magnitude of the steady-state current flowing through the circuit." :tutorial/id #uuid "e19d072d-824c-5014-9eaf-0cde5336358b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14580 :tutorial/title "Steady-State Solutions for Sinusoidal EMFs"} :step/id #uuid "1071dff8-b74d-5864-ade1-84d518b3c702" :step/math-academy-id 589215 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120948}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120948 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119964 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119543 :question/id #uuid "2594ae82-9bd8-5ee4-a5a9-4be27b5d9097" :question/math-academy-id "e-21761"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050392}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441932}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"} :step/id #uuid "9457cf05-1395-5f80-89f3-a0f5a2865931" :step/math-academy-id 589216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120960 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120989 :tutorial/content "Electrical circuits are said to be **in series** if the components are connected end to end in a single path, so that the same current flows through every component in the circuit.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll look at **RL circuits**, which contain both a resistor and an inductor, most commonly in series with a voltage source. An example is shown below, consisting of a resistance $R,$ in ohms, an inductance $L,$ in henries, and a source EMF $E,$ in volts. \n\n![](images/6c/6c91b527a400a918ca14133c5fb7aa713c6d53cb7dde3e0619ab82f1a846e092.png)\n\nHow do we find a differential equation that best describes the circuit's current $I(t),$ measured in amperes, where $t$ is the time, in seconds?\n\nAccording to conservation of energy, as electric charge moves around a circuit, the energy gained from the source must equal the energy lost in the components. The decrease in voltage across a component is called a **voltage drop**.\n\nFrom this follows **Kirchhoff's Voltage Law**, which states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops around the loop.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The resistor *opposes the flow of current*, hence producing a voltage drop proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R{:}$ \n\n$$\nV_R = RI\n$$\n\n- The inductor *opposes changes in current*, hence producing a voltage drop proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L{:}$ \n\n$$\nV_L = L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's Voltage Law, the source voltage $E$ is the sum of the voltage drops: $E = V_R + V_L.$\n\nAs a result, the basic equation governing the amount of current $I(t)$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\nE = RI + L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{R}{L}I = \\dfrac{E}{L}.\n$$\n\nNext, we will look at a worked example with specific values for $R,$ $L,$ and $E.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e3e37bc6-6596-57dd-b9d7-4e1d03a5beec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14899 :tutorial/title "RL Circuits"} :step/id #uuid "6bd5d887-600b-5c39-9f37-c936cf2ce2ef" :step/math-academy-id 608075 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120943}}] :activity/title "Modeling RL Circuits With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120008}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486490} :activity/id #uuid "2dbd1490-4230-5bac-885e-7ac22a371c92" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051040 :topic/id #uuid "7c8d2ba6-2860-5111-b509-9ce2c7360cf0" :topic/math-academy-id 1788} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486490 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9d91d0e-8b5a-5632-a09f-535db58568d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117723} {:answer/id #uuid "cc646d14-0593-5dec-b288-3369a0fa5bf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117724} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d4f391-fa26-5de9-ab15-9317b19d9ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117725} {:answer/id #uuid "02ac43f8-5308-5230-b8ef-8bacf1094585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117726} {:answer/id #uuid "794cc58a-b1a0-54c6-a20d-36ee52d2e2dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117727}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186117725} :answer-field/id #uuid "b02dc0f6-e80f-5cb8-badb-b415dfadbdf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d82ca13b-67a8-5070-a065-c95927f9b6d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-336463" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea1eb590e18a85672f176188f1bab96d85eb1be92d43edd322d1d032500a97ac.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-14t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $7\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=7\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=7I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=7I+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+14I=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+14{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-14t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+14(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2(-\\alpha +7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2(7\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-\\alpha +7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(7\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\alpha +7\\beta &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 7\\alpha +\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{50},$ $\\beta =\\frac{21}{50}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{21}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-14t}-\\frac{3}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{21}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{50},\\,\\beta =\\frac{21}{50}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{3}{50}+\\frac{21}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7b1b56dd-b9e3-5d01-ac4e-820cdf14cd96" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486509}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486501 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0611168e-01b6-5eb5-9bdd-eb0c4ed87d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{44}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308796} {:answer/id #uuid "6b10d57c-e7e9-5a1a-9212-88743ad9bdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309579} {:answer/id #uuid "76c13a1a-1f33-531d-aa9c-39874842b71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309676} {:answer/id #uuid "c40b2d25-a48d-5c41-8009-e4d5ff3d8337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310320} {:answer/id #uuid "da9e72d4-e5ec-5e8d-a454-39ca5f817a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{42}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310503}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310503} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ea618d5-3a70-51f3-bce5-1d4b0166d37f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "195c82a8-9f40-5bf0-8acd-c9d6e6363995" :question/math-academy-id "q-336741" :question/problem "![](images/00/00d6676b631bd49c0c9b8a82f8dd05de3be6842f18b0c1c6b70f208e1d290d5a.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-12t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)=6I+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+12{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-12t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $5t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $5t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+5\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))+12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ (-5\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+(12\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha +12\\beta &=6\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 12\\alpha +5\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{30}{169},$ $\\beta =\\frac{72}{169}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{30}{169}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{72}{169}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-12t}-\\frac{30}{169}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{72}{169}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{30}{169},\\,\\beta =\\frac{72}{169}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{30}{169}+\\frac{72}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{42}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a7db3e8f-89ed-5472-b6be-333d89d420fc"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486508 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e608fb46-d69b-55d4-a66f-af2bca92a26f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486186} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bceb32f-2521-5ed4-a561-957b01d5ba94" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3d5697b5-d337-5d01-ae32-ec8e66fe10a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-336655" :question/problem "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $20\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $4\\,\\text{H}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the circuit's current after $0.02$ seconds given that the initial current is $2\\,\\text{A}.$\n\nThe current after $0.02$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8=20I+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=2.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+5{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+5\\alpha =2\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $2\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=2,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =2 \\\\ A{e}^{-5(0)}+\\frac{2}{5} & =2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{8}{5}{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(4{e}^{-5t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $t=0.02$ seconds is\n\n$$\nI(0.02)=\\frac{2}{5}(4{e}^{-5(0.02)}+1)≈1.85\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "c7e27098-8d0e-5ee5-96b4-0f581c64acfd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486501}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486509 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6d9eb43-6151-57e3-b17e-712357ab932f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310228} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7f5c91-01db-574e-8416-7292a1715248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485818} :answer-field/id #uuid "43600dac-6d25-5682-8937-5005846b9935" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "92ff7b67-920f-5e25-a5d9-2aca35a6388f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336643" :question/problem "The current $I(t),$ in amperes, in a series RL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=3,\\,I(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state current for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state current is\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "The equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+6{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+6\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}=A{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${I}_{p}=\\frac{1}{2}$ is the *steady-state current*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the current $I$ approaches the value of the steady-state current.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state current is $\\frac{1}{2}$ amperes."} :step/id #uuid "cda8dc57-f964-532b-9b39-778d1de7603c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486520}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2deb0dc-4abd-518c-854a-8134786e852f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377227} :answer-field/id #uuid "2791feb8-6710-5d48-88a6-a2f36341287f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5c397fb9-bf38-5a44-8a25-4f5a4f9ab367" :question/math-academy-id "q-336652" :question/problem "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$ Find, **rounded to two decimal places**, the circuit's current after $0.02$ seconds given that the initial current is $1\\,\\text{A}.$\n\nThe current after $0.02$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n6=30I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=2.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+10{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+10\\alpha =2\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-10(0)}+\\frac{1}{5} & =1 \\\\ A & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{1}{5}(4{e}^{-10t}+1)\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $t=0.02$ seconds is\n\n$$\nI(0.02)=\\frac{1}{5}(4{e}^{-10(0.02)}+1)≈0.85\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "f83669e7-ed4c-5c9e-be92-12862cff41bd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486508}}] :activity/title "Modeling RL Circuits With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485751}]}
